course: chinese-street
lesson: 25
title: Spring Festival — Chinese New Year
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Chūnjié, the Spring Festival, is Chinese New Year: it falls in late January or February by the moon calendar, so its date moves every year, like Easter, and families travel huge distances to be together.
explain: The night before, chúxī, New Year's Eve, is the big one: the whole family folds dumplings together (bāo jiǎozi), eats the reunion dinner (niányèfàn), watches the TV Gala (Chūnwǎn) and tries to stay up past midnight (shǒusuì).
explain: An old story says "nián" was a monster scared away by the colour red and loud bangs — so there are red couplets by the door, red lanterns and firecrackers.
explain: Watch for the upside-down "fú" (luck) sign on the door: it is upside down on purpose, because dào (upside down) sounds like dào (arrived), so "fú dào le" means "luck has arrived" — don't "fix" it!
explain: The one wish everybody needs is "xīnnián kuàilè!" (SHIN-nyen KWIGH-luh), Happy New Year — say it to every member of Lín's family the moment you walk in.
fact: "Chūnjié" means the Spring Festival, Chinese New Year; it falls in late January or February by the lunar calendar, not on 1 January.
fact: "guònián" means to celebrate New Year, literally "pass the year"; "nǐmen zěnme guònián?" asks how a family celebrates.
fact: In an old story "nián" was a monster scared away by red and by loud bangs.
fact: "xīnnián kuàilè" means Happy New Year and is said to everyone at Chinese New Year and also on 1 January.
fact: "chúxī" means New Year's Eve, the most important evening; in some homes nobody sweeps the floor that day, so as not to sweep the luck away.
fact: "niányèfàn" is the New Year's Eve reunion dinner, the biggest meal of the year; being invited is an honour, so dress tidily, greet every elder and try everything.
fact: "bāo jiǎozi" means making dumplings; families fold them together on New Year's Eve, and a coin hidden in one brings luck to whoever bites it.
fact: "fàng biānpào" means setting off firecrackers, at midnight in China and at Chinatown parades in Britain; firecrackers are for grown-ups, and the noise can scare small kids and dogs.
fact: "chūnlián" are red New Year couplets, poems on red paper put up by the door before New Year.
fact: "fú dào le" means "luck has arrived"; the fú sign is hung upside down on purpose, because dào (upside down) sounds like dào (arrived).
fact: "wǔ shī" is the lion dance at parades and shop openings; the lion "eats" lettuce and red envelopes hung up by shops.
fact: "Chūnwǎn" is the Spring Festival TV Gala, which families watch on New Year's Eve; grown-ups complain about it every year and still watch every minute.
fact: "shǒusuì" means staying up to see in the New Year; children are allowed up past midnight, and falling asleep first is the classic.
exercise: When is Chūnjié? | Late January or February, by the moon | Always on 1 January, same as ours | Always on 25 December | In the middle of summer, in July
exercise: Why does the date of Chinese New Year move every year? | It follows the lunar calendar | The government picks a new date | It's on the first Monday of the year | It follows the school holidays
exercise: Lín asks "nǐmen zěnme guònián?". What is he asking? | How does your family celebrate New Year? | When is your birthday this year? | Where does your family live now? | What did you eat today?
exercise: In the old story, what scared away the monster nián? | Red colour and loud bangs | Bright moonlight over the village | Buckets of cold water | Sweet cakes
exercise: You arrive at Lín's on New Year's Day. What do you say to everyone? | xīnnián kuàilè! | shēngrì kuàilè! | zàijiàn! | wǎn'ān!
exercise: What is chúxī? | New Year's Eve | New Year's Day | The Lantern Festival | The Moon Festival
exercise: Lín's granny won't let you sweep the floor on chúxī. Why? | You'd sweep the luck away | The broom is broken again | She's just tired of the noise | Sweeping is for men only
exercise: What is niányèfàn? | The New Year's Eve reunion dinner | A breakfast noodle for the first day | A New Year song | A firework show over the river
exercise: You're invited to Lín's niányèfàn. How should you behave? | Dress tidily, greet the elders, try everything | Wear football kit and eat only crisps | Greet only Lín and go off to play games | Arrive late and leave early
exercise: The whole family is "bāo jiǎozi" at the table. What are they doing? | Folding dumplings | Wrapping presents | Writing couplets | Hanging lanterns
exercise: You bite a dumpling and find a coin! What does it mean? | Good luck for you | You must pay for dinner | The cook made a mistake | You've lost a tooth
exercise: "fàng biānpào" at the Chinatown parade — what's happening? | Setting off firecrackers | A lion is dancing down the street | People are singing | A dragon boat race on the river
exercise: Lín's dad sticks red paper with poems by the front door. What are they? | chūnlián, New Year couplets | hóngbāo, red envelopes for kids | dēnglong, lanterns | Chūnwǎn, the big TV show
exercise: The fú sign on Lín's door is upside down. What do you do? | Leave it: upside down means luck arrived | Turn it the right way up, carefully | Tell Lín's mum it fell down | Take it down
exercise: At a Chinatown parade a big lion bobs along eating lettuce. What is it? | wǔ shī, the lion dance | fàng biānpào, firecrackers | sài lóngzhōu, boat race | shǎng yuè, moon-watching
exercise: Lín's grandparents never miss Chūnwǎn. What is it? | The Spring Festival TV Gala | The New Year's Eve family dinner | The dumpling-folding contest | The lion dance
exercise: Lín's little sister wants to do shǒusuì. What does she want? | To stay up past midnight | To go to bed early for once | To eat ten dumplings | To set off the firecrackers
test: my friend says Chinese New Year is on 1 Jan like ours but they just party more. right? | No, it's late Jan or Feb, by the moon | Yes, same day, just a much bigger party | Yes, but only in China | No, it's always in March, in spring
test: You "fix" the upside-down fú sign on Lín's door to be helpful. What have you done? | Undone the "luck has arrived" pun | Made it luckier for the whole year | Found a secret message | Nothing, it doesn't matter which way up
test: Lín's cousin, aged 5, wants to light firecrackers at the party. What's right? | Firecrackers are for grown-ups only | Let him, it's lucky for little ones | Let him if he wears something red | Let him if granny watches TV
test: Lín's mum moans every year "Chūnwǎn is so boring!" and then watches every minute. Is that strange? | It's a famous family habit, everyone does it | She must be lying about it being boring | She is forced to by law | It means she loves boring things
test: What would happen if Lín's grandad swept the floor hard on New Year's Eve, in a family that keeps the custom? | They'd joke he'd swept the luck away | They'd say the luck had doubled for the year | They'd give him a red envelope for it | Nothing, it's normal
test: Lín's sister falls asleep on the sofa at 10 pm on chúxī. What has she failed? | shǒusuì, staying up for midnight | bāo jiǎozi, folding dumplings | wǔ shī, the lion dance | niányèfàn, the dinner
test: A friend says the red everywhere at New Year is just because Chinese people like red. What does the old story add? | Red scared away the monster nián | Red means danger, so keep away from the house | Red is cheap in winter | Red is for weddings only, not New Year
test: In a cartoon the family eats the reunion dinner on New Year's Day morning. What's wrong? | niányèfàn is on New Year's Eve | Nothing, it's breakfast | It should be at Easter | It should be at the Moon Festival
test: The lion dancer comes to a shop and grabs a red envelope hung with lettuce. Is the lion stealing? | No, the lion "eats" them: it's part of the dance | Yes, call the police, it's daylight robbery | Yes, but it's allowed once a year | No, he's buying lettuce
test: Which two things happen at the same table on New Year's Eve? | bāo jiǎozi and niányèfàn | wǔ shī and fàng biānpào | Chūnwǎn and shǎng yuè | sài lóngzhōu and zòngzi
test: Your mum asks what to say to Lín's family at New Year. What's the one wish everyone needs? | xīnnián kuàilè | shēngrì kuàilè | zhōumò kuàilè | wǎn'ān
exam: Identify the Mandarin designation of the festival known in English as Chinese New Year. | Chūnjié | Zhōngqiū jié | Duānwǔ jié | Qīngmíng jié
exam: It is given that the character for "fortune" is affixed inverted upon a door. Select the statement that accurately characterises the reason. | dào (inverted) sounds like dào (arrived): fortune has arrived | It was affixed in error by careless persons | Inversion averts the attention of thieves from the household | Inversion denotes mourning
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "niányèfàn"? | The family reunion dinner held on New Year's Eve | A breakfast eaten on the first day of spring | A meal eaten at the Moon Festival by the family | A banquet held on one's birthday
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the custom designated "shǒusuì". | Remaining awake to witness the arrival of the New Year | Abstaining from food on New Year's Day until nightfall | Visiting the graves of ancestors | Racing boats upon a river
